# Implement the direct Proton Mail adapter

## Goal

Provide the version-one Mail provider path using authority established by the
session-fork login flow.

## Required work

- Use only session authority produced by the accepted fork lifecycle.
- Require an `Approved` `ProviderRequestIdentity` on every live provider
  request; never reconstruct `x-pm-appversion` from raw configuration text.
- Implement bounded timeouts, cancellation, retry/backoff, refresh, pagination,
  concurrency control, and structured provider error conversion.
- Convert Proton messages, conversations, labels, drafts, events, and
  attachments into provider-neutral domain types.
- Preserve Proton's client-side cryptographic behavior needed to read and send
  mail while minimizing plaintext/key lifetime.
- Detect protocol incompatibility and fail closed instead of returning partial
  or mis-threaded data.

## Acceptance criteria

- No Bridge, automated browser, or account-password fallback exists in the
  supported path.
- The live transport obtains both `x-pm-appversion` and API base only through
  `ProviderRequestIdentity` live accessors and rejects `None` before network
  I/O.
- Every advertised capability has direct protocol fixture evidence or is omitted
  during negotiation.
- Provider-specific URLs, tokens, encrypted payloads, and key material do not
  cross into capability-domain or MCP schemas.
- Refresh and remote revocation behave deterministically.
- A gated live account can execute every capability advertised by this adapter.

## Evidence

- Direct adapter contract and protocol-fixture tests
- Capability-negotiation matrix
- Session refresh/revocation regression tests
- Provider-change fail-closed fixture
- Redacted opt-in live-account transcript
